<!-- page 5 -->

Transformative AI and the implications for Tax and Legal

AI, labour and economics:
The impact on taxation

Governments have long depended on taxation of labour and income to raise revenue. But as AI is poised to disrupt jobs and displace human workers, how will this impact future tax revenue?

Already in the software industry, coding that used to require days or weeks of labour can be completed quickly by AI agents. Humans have shifted their focus to conceiving programming tasks, monitoring, and checking results. Some employers have announced cuts in software jobs, and surveys suggest that many employers across a range of industries believe they will be eliminating jobs in AI-exposed roles.¹²

AI’s expanding capabilities have inspired predictions that vast numbers of entry-level white-collar jobs could be eliminated in the next few years.¹³ Other types of jobs might also be impacted as AI is combined with robotic automation and Physical AI—making it possible to automate even more-manual tasks.

Complex variables come into play in any prediction of how AI might have an impact on jobs, coloured by the experience of past technological leaps that both eliminated certain types of work and created whole new areas of employment. In some cases where AI automates work, the employee may become more productive and valuable by overseeing the AI as it completes tasks—for gains in output more than loss of jobs.¹⁴

Ethan Mollick, a professor at the Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania, has described the growth of AI capabilities as the expansion of a “jagged frontier.” The AI may be very good in one area, such as computer coding tasks, but may be surprisingly slow to gain needed skills in other areas, creating a check on job losses.¹⁵ There will also be resistance to allowing AI to handle certain tasks due to organisational inertia or societal decisions, even when the technology can handle them.¹⁶

05

---

<!-- page 6 -->

Reshaping of the tax base

The heart of the tax matter, though, as certain types of labour are eliminated or devalued, as employment shrinks or average wages fall, is that systems built around individual income taxes, payroll taxes, and social security contributions will be challenged. The labour part of the tax base could enter a long period of erosion, requiring that other revenue sources be tapped.

Taxation of corporate profits may also be disrupted by AI’s economic shifts. As companies generate revenue and profit growth without proportional labour costs, more value will come from capital and investments in AI systems. The geographic attribution of income—deciding where economic activity actually takes place—may become more difficult. Existing transfer pricing and permanent establishment rules may be strained.

Overall, the economic impacts of AI may require redesigns of tax systems that are more structural than incremental. There is growing discussion of the possibility of taxing AI tokens, the basic unit processed by large language models, or taxing data centres.¹⁷ Options once seen as theoretical may move to the mainstream, such as taxing the productive capacity of AI agents rather than the profits they generate or shifting the tax base from profits to revenue or gross income.

Governments may face the need for higher social safety net spending as AI impacts labour—and pressure to ensure that wealth created by AI is distributed fairly across society. Novel ideas for how wealth created by transformative AI could be shared more broadly include sovereign AI funds, universal basic income or even something called universal basic capital, which would make people stakeholders in AI-driven wealth creation.¹⁸

Tax systems change slowly, with governments struggling to keep up with the pace of change. On the other hand, AI may help to enable the kind of transformation that’s needed, making it feasible for policymakers to more rapidly and effectively model tax scenarios or highlight contradictions in existing tax rules. This could create more opportunities for collaboration, consultation and effective policymaking.

Fundamentally, AI calls into question long-held assumptions about how value is created and where profits are recognised. This will require policymakers to dispense with orthodoxy and think creatively about whole new societal and economic constructs and their implications.

06

