<!-- page 7 -->

Transformative AI and the implications for Tax and Legal

AI, acceleration of the digital administration of tax

In the administration of tax, there is an existing path for increasing automation and digitalisation. The Tax Administration 3.0 framework from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) has, since its initial publication in 2020, helped to drive digital transformation, with a particular emphasis on more seamless data sharing between the systems of tax authorities and taxpayers.

The increased use of AI in tax fits well with the long-run transformation journey envisioned in Tax Administration 3.0. Research by the OECD has found that more than 70% of tax authorities already apply AI to at least some tasks, including fraud detection, compliance risk assessment, and taxpayer services (OECD 2025). This in turn creates pressure on taxpayers to implement AI tools in part to keep pace with tax authorities.

Taxpayers are indeed moving forward with changes in their systems to boost the use of AI—and reap the benefits. Some 46% of respondents in Deloitte’s Tax Transformation Trends 2025 survey say they have implemented AI-driven tax analysis tools, while most of the remaining respondents say they are planning to¹⁹.

There is a jagged frontier surrounding AI capabilities in tax, to be sure. The technology is making big advances in some areas but still not ready for deployment on other tasks. Some past examples show how poorly applied algorithms and automated decisions can lead to biased outcomes, as in the scandal over penalties related to childcare subsidies wrongly applied to thousands of parents in the Netherlands. As opaque AI models are used for tax audits or enforcement, the explainability of automated decisions will be increasingly important.

This is a disruptive moment as AI is embedded into enterprise systems and the tax function becomes better integrated with the real-time data flows of the business itself. As AI drives change in the functioning of businesses and the economy, the underlying facts tax relies upon will also change. AI doesn’t just automate tax, it changes how companies operate, and this shift will raise new issues related to value creation, cost structures and operational profiles—tax will need to react.

