<!-- page 8 -->

Transformative AI and the implications for Tax and Legal

AI and the operation of law

AI is poised to change the law in many ways. At the most fundamental level, the operation of law may be pushed by transformative AI from ex post, the after-the-fact litigation and enforcement that characterises the system today, to ex ante, with rules embedded in the systems where transactions happen, enabling earlier detection and intervention.

More immediately, the legal field is already seeing changes in how routine work gets done. AI can tackle time-consuming tasks related to contract and document review, legal discovery, and the drafting of briefs and documents. While there have been some high-profile missteps involving unwise use of AI for court filings and inadequate reviews, many routine legal tasks increasingly fall within AI’s capability frontier.

Advances in the ability of AI to do legal work are shifting the economics of legal practices and disrupting lawyers’ career paths. Deeper impacts are coming—in litigation, law enforcement and criminal justice more broadly—as AI creates opportunities for continuous monitoring, embedded compliance, and automated enforcement decision-making.

Moving toward law as code

Ultimately, AI-driven change may call into question the premise that legislation and regulation should primarily be delivered as human-readable text—authored by legislatures and agencies, interpreted by courts, circulated through existing publishing and software systems. The end-state the legal system may be headed toward is sometimes called law as code.²⁰

Embracing law as code would create a world in which regulation is delivered as part of the digital infrastructure of the economy—in systems for tax compliance, in the monitoring of bank transactions for money laundering, in supply chain contracts, and much more. There is clear appeal in having laws and rules encoded into systems that calculate and validate transactions.

Greater automation in the application of law, and an eventual embrace of law as code, offers significant positives in areas such as access to justice. Today, civil legal needs dramatically outstrip available and affordable legal help, for example. This is an area where broad use of AI could change the game—bringing down the unit cost of legal information and legal work.

AI has the potential to turn much of the existing work in the application of law from a time-intensive process of production into one of mostly human supervision, strategy, and judgment. The path from today’s legal system, fully dependent on natural language laws and regulations, to this coded future is unclear, however. Law as code remains a long-term goal, even as AI-driven changes to legal practice and careers arrive much more quickly.

08

