<!-- page 9 -->

Transformative AI and the implications for Tax and Legal

AI and the tax and legal professions

The idea that work in professions steeped in the acquisition and application of bodies of knowledge is protected from automation has already been upended by AI’s recent advances. Entry-level work in white-collar fields, certainly including tax and law, appears to already be disrupted, though the speed at which career paths will shift remains unclear.

The future state for the tax and legal professions may see its workers become agentic product owners, orchestrating AI systems rather than executing tasks directly. The human role shifts to oversight, validation, and strategic direction, and leaders in the professions envision a hybrid human-AI model for the workforce.

The increased efficiency in tax and legal work that AI promises may help businesses to be more self-sufficient, using new technology to complete more legal work in-house. On the other hand, it may lead them to rely more on AI agents and systems powered by advisers, embedded into their own systems but owned elsewhere.

Already, companies are devoting resources to AI for tax and legal work. The venture capital firm Andreessen Horowitz estimates that AI startups focused on legal offerings already are recording about $500 million a year in revenue.¹ The analysis suggests that legal is an area where companies are eager to adopt AI, perhaps second only to software coding in terms of momentum for the use case.

What remains to be addressed is where accountability and liability will rest when more tax and legal professional work is handed off to AI tools. When there are incorrect filings, misapplied tax rules, faulty legal briefs, or missed legal precedents, few will accept that the tools alone can be blamed. What’s more, work cannot simply seem to come from a black box, without clear reasoning, audit trails, and defensible interpretations of regulations and laws.

The central challenge in the tax and legal professions is not technological but institutional. More advanced adoption of AI tools requires a rethinking of existing systems of professional responsibility—which today rest primarily in the qualifications and certifications of the people who work in these fields. How that transition is managed will determine not just the efficiency gains from AI, but the integrity and trustworthiness of the professions.

09

